.TH MKDIR 1.SH NAMEmkdir \- make directories.SH SYNOPSIS\fBmkdir\fR[\-p] [\-m mode] dir ....SH DESCRIPTION\fBmkdir\fRis GNU mkdir, from the fileutils.  It makes each named directory.  Adirectory that already exists is an error, reported as\fBcreating an existing file\fRwith exit status 1..SH OPTIONS.TP 12\fB\-p\fRmake every missing directory on the way as well:\fBmkdir -p a/b/c\fRmakes all three, and a directory that already exists is no longer anerror.  Long form\fB+path\fR.\fR.TP 12\fB\-m\fI mode\fRset the new directory's permissions from a Unix-style octal\fImode\fR.\fROS-9's attributes are not Unix modes, and\fB\-m 700\fRhere left a directory with no permissions at all, so check the resultwith\fBattr\fRor\fBls \-l\fR,\fRor leave\fB\-m\fRout.  Long form\fB+mode \fImode\fR.\fR.SH EXAMPLES.nfmkdir notes                one directorymkdir -p tmp/work/today    and everything above it.fi.SH NOTESNeeds Microware's\fBcio\fR.\fROS-9's own directory maker is\fBmakdir\fR,\fRso the two names do not collide..SH SEE ALSOrm, attr